## Part II · Equities — more than twenty studies, each with its autopsy and its number

### Three eras, and how the method learned not to fool itself

Twenty-one studies across three eras, in which the method matured along the way. None with an edge that survived costs.

#### First era: I thought I had it (measured, not yet sealed)

| Study | The number that closed it | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| The hunt that followed the sun: pre-market gappers | 53 % hit rate against a 42 % base; 21,730 gappers, 263 combinations | no signal |
| · intraday: does the morning peak keep rising? | −0.35 % per trade | dies (sealed afterwards) |
| · structure: do age, splits or type separate them? | −0.13 %; the positive part was June | dies (sealed afterwards) |
| · short pressure: does short crowding predict? | it was liquidity, not shorts: −0.18 % | dies (sealed afterwards) |
| World markets (the pattern outside the US) | 0 tradable rules in 7 exchanges | explored |
| Rebound after a trading halt (halt-resume) | real profit factor 1.04; the 12.6 was a mirage from looking at the future | no edge |
| 21,384 trading halts | best AUC 0.58 (a coin: 0.50) | no edge |
| Clean gap-up: follow the opening jump | 1 hit in 20, apparent; an illusion | dies |
| Gap reversal: bet the jump unwinds | −1.40 % per trade (median) | dies |
| Sector contagion | the signal does not cover the cost | dies |

The hunt that followed the sun was explored before the discipline existed: it was documented and sealed after seeing the ground. A late seal does not turn a finding into a prediction; it only stops anyone, the author included, from rewriting afterwards what was done. The three pre-market veins were closed at the end with the same sealed system.

#### Second era: the contract signed before looking

| Family | The number that killed it | Signed |
|---|---|---|
| A · Real cost | tool: median spread 0.06 %; at the open, between 0.3 % and 0.4 % | no (tool) |
| B · Aggressor flow | −0.035 % per trade: signal ~0.01 % against cost ~0.06 %; the placebo matches it | yes |
| C · Late momentum | the out-of-sample gain came from a single day, when 87 % of stocks rose | yes |
| D · Breadth | explains 0.6 % of the move | yes |
| E · After earnings | −0.73 % against the control | yes |
| F · Entering the index | 96 % of the effect was momentum | yes |
| G · Turn of the month | signal +0.17 %, placebo +0.19 % per trade (S&P 500, 2016–2026; check 2005–2015) | yes |
| I · Trend following | +0.81 % a month against +1.17 % for buy and hold | yes |
| J · Microcaps | median −2.56 % per trade; the mean, +0.11 %, is inflated by a few jumps | yes |

Turn of the month: the rule made 2 % a year while buying the index and doing nothing made 13.25 % a year over the same period. Proving such a small effect against so much noise would take about two hundred years of data.

#### Third era: the last attempt, over several days (signed)

| Family | The number that killed it | Signed |
|---|---|---|
| K · Regime (200-day average) | gives up 4.6 points a year; not worth it | yes |
| R · Regime detector | 1.9 false alarms a year (maximum: 1) | yes |

Across the three eras: ten studies with a contract signed and sealed before looking, plus the three pre-market veins, closed at the end with the same system. The first-era studies are measured but unsealed.

Three mirages repeat themselves:

- The tide: when the whole market rises, any strategy that buys looks smart.
- Momentum: what was already rising tends to rise a little more; many "new signals" were that old effect under another name.
- The spectacular case: a few huge jumps that inflate the average.

And under all three, the toll: once the mirages were removed and the cost was paid, nothing was left.

### The bill, to the cent

The equities programme cost about €2,000 in total. In crypto, €0, because the data was free. What was earned, in both markets: zero.

